WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.640
David Fincher's psychological thriller Fight
Club was an enigmatic ride that still leaves

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.059
audiences scratching their heads upon review.

00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:11.870
The 1999 pic ended with a twist and a bang
— and undermined everything we thought we

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.240
saw before the final act.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.970
To clear up any lingering confusion about
this modern classic, here's a breakdown of

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.230
what actually happened at the end of Fight
Club.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.860
Will the real Tyler Durden please stand up?

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.619
Early on in Fight Club, we're introduced to
Brad Pitt as Tyler Durden, a buff, tough,

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:27.970
and rough guy who lives and breathes chaos.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.019
"How's that working out for you?"

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:33.580
He befriends our sleepless Narrator, played
by Edward Norton, and the two cook up a plan

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.520
to wage war on the ills of capitalism.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.400
Only, as we discover in the final act, Tyler
isn't real.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.070
He's the human embodiment of a frustrated
corporate drone's desperate yearning to destroy

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.730
the system that's confined him.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:47.160
If Fight Club were shot from a third-person
perspective, it would be the story of a mentally

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.430
unbalanced man leading a bizarre double life.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.150
By day, he's a bored office worker.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.280
By night, he's a charismatic cult leader who
likes to punch things.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.150
But thanks to the movie's unreliable first-person
narrative, we see Tyler like the Narrator

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.740
sees him: as a charming nihilist who gets
the girl.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.940
It takes Norton's character a while to figure
everything out, but eventually he starts to

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.300
realize that he's Tyler …

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.300
"What'd you just call me?

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.240
Say my name."

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.660
"Tyler Durden, Tyler Durden."

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.760
That's when he confronts Tyler — and discovers
that he created this man in his own image

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.170
… or at least the image of what he wants
to be.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.040
"All the ways you wish you could be, that's
me."

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.940
"I am free in all the ways that you are not."

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.380
So, basically, Tyler is a hallucinatory hitman,
hired by the Narrator's subconscious to blow

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.650
up his life … and a few other things along
the way.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.030
All the clues

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.740
Fight Club doesn't tip its hand much when
it comes to foreshadowing its big twist, but

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.630
Tyler Durden actually appears on four separate
occasions before we ever really meet him — in

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.820
the form of single-frame blips on the screen.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.000
On re-watch, these moments are meant to be
clues that Tyler isn't a real person.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.799
And it's no coincidence that our first glimpses
of Tyler come in the form of subliminal messages,

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:51.700
since he liked to add subliminal private parts
to movies in his night job as a projectionist.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.229
By the time he makes his grand entrance as
the Narrator's airplane seatmate, you kinda

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:56.560
feel like you know him.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.810
"Nobody knows that they saw it, but they did."

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:03.030
One of Fight Club's niftier tricks is that
despite being the film's two central characters,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.490
Tyler and the Narrator almost never interact
with each other in front of anyone else.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.340
But one notable exception is a moment that
takes place about two thirds of the way through,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.590
when they're in a car crash along with two
other members of Project Mayhem.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.160
On first watch, this scene seems like a couple's
spat of sorts between the Narrator and Tyler,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.730
with the Mechanic and Steph playing the part
of a bizarre Greek chorus in the background.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.200
But if you delete the Narrator's half of the
dialogue, the scene still totally works as

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.940
an illustration of indoctrination-in-action.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.740
"Let go!"

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.920
After the accident, it's worth noting that
Tyler emerges from the passenger side and

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.060
pulls the Narrator from the driver's seat,
showing, once again, that Tyler was never

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.800
really there.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.140
We need to talk about Marla

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.460
As far as the Narrator is concerned, Marla
is a nuisance and an interloper whose noisy

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.410
sexual relationship with Tyler keeps him awake
all night.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.989
But from Marla's point of view, the Narrator
and Tyler are one and the same — which actually

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:55.430
goes a long way toward explaining why she
continues to maintain a relationship with

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.430
him.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.160
The Tyler Durden Marla knows is moody, emotionally
unavailable, and kind of a jerk to her.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.219
As far as Marla is concerned, though, Tyler
is a perfectly ordinary breed of bad boyfriend

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.190
— and one who's good enough in bed to be
worth the continued emotional investment.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.980
"You met me at a very strange time in my life."

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.730
After all, they started out as just a couple
of group therapy tourists, so they were equally

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.930
messed up from the get-go.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.489
Is Tyler dead?

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:22.569
In the final moments of Fight Club, the Narrator
decides to rid himself of Tyler once and for

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.120
all — by shooting himself.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.629
By doing so, he simultaneously takes and surrenders
control, and is reborn into one whole self.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:33.440
"It's only after we've lost everything that
we're free to do anything."

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.879
For all intents and purposes, Tyler Durden
as the Narrator knew him is dead, and the

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:40.330
real Tyler is free to be whoever he wants
to be.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.340
With Marla at his side, he watches calmly
as Project Mayhem reaches its inevitable conclusion.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:49.330
But the point of Project Mayhem was to destroy
all debts so that civilization could start

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.569
anew without everyone being so beholden to
consumerism.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:57.830
"Advertising has us chasing cars and clothes,
working jobs we hate so we can buy s--- we

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:58.980
don't need."

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.940
And since this was just the first of many
groups going forward with the plan for apocalyptic

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:07.459
destruction to dismantle the system, after
the credits roll, Tyler Durden's legacy definitely

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.520
lives on — for good or for ill.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.300
[Gunshot]
"Whoa!

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:14.120
Whoa!"

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.419
Thanks for watching!

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:18.489
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:20.910
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

